            "presentation_state": "show_all",
            "properties": {
                "0": {
                    "name": "block",
                    "value": "Fills a tensor with random samples from the LOG-NORMAL distribution - always positive, bunched low, with a long tail - keeping the tensor's shape.\n\nEach element becomes e raised to the power of a bell-curve sample, so the LOGARITHMS of the values form a bell. Use it for positive random sizes, durations or gains the size of data you already have. To make the samples without an input tensor, use t.dist.log_normal.\n\nSYNTAX:\nt.log_normal\nThe node takes no arguments.\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\ntensor in:\nThe tensor to fill. Only its SHAPE, dtype and device are used - its values are ignored, and it is not changed (the node fills a copy). A list or number is turned into a tensor first. Every tensor that arrives is filled and sent; this is the only inlet that makes the node send. The input must have a floating-point dtype (float32, the default for the t.* generators, is right). An integer tensor - from t.zeros with dtype int64, say - prints an error in the console and nothing is sent.\n\nmean (drag float, also an inlet):\nThe mean of the LOGARITHMS, not of the values: the median value is e to the power mean. Default 0 (a median of 1).\n\nstd (drag float, also an inlet):\nThe spread of the logarithms. Must be above 0; 0 or below is an error (printed in the console) and nothing is sent. Default 1.\n\nThe parameters are drag floats on the node and also inlets. They are read when the next tensor arrives; setting one does not send. In the demo each slider goes through 't bang float', which passes the number to the parameter first and THEN clicks the t.zeros, so a fresh tensor arrives and is filled at once.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\ntensor out:\nA new tensor, the same shape, dtype and device as the input, filled with fresh independent samples.\n\nTHE HISTOGRAM:\nt.histc divides the range from its min to its max into equal bins and counts how many of the 4096 samples land in each; the bar plot draws the counts, the first bin at the left. Its shape is the shape of the distribution. Samples outside min..max are not counted at all.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\nload_bang clicks t.zeros once when the page opens, and its 64 x 64 tensor of zeros comes back from the node as 4096 samples; the zeros themselves never show, only their shape matters. The heat_map (0 dark, 5 bright) is mostly dark with bright specks. The histogram rises steeply from 0, peaks below 1 and trails off to the right. Raise mean and the shape stretches out; lower std and the tail shortens.\n\nRELATED:\nt.dist.log_normal makes the same samples from a typed shape. t.normal fills with the bell this is built on; t.exponential is another positive fill.",
                    "value_type": "str"
                },
                "1": {
                    "name": "lock",
                    "value": true,
                    "value_type": "bool"
                },
                "2": {
                    "name": "width",
                    "value": 760,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "3": {
                    "name": "height",
                    "value": 955,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "4": {
                    "name": "text_size",
                    "value": "30",
                    "value_type": "str"
                }
            },
            "text": "Fills a tensor with random samples from the LOG-NORMAL distribution - always positive, bunched low, with a long tail - keeping the tensor's shape.\n\nEach element becomes e raised to the power of a bell-curve sample, so the LOGARITHMS of the values form a bell. Use it for positive random sizes, durations or gains the size of data you already have. To make the samples without an input tensor, use t.dist.log_normal.\n\nSYNTAX:\nt.log_normal\nThe node takes no arguments.\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\ntensor in:\nThe tensor to fill. Only its SHAPE, dtype and device are used - its values are ignored, and it is not changed (the node fills a copy). A list or number is turned into a tensor first. Every tensor that arrives is filled and sent; this is the only inlet that makes the node send. The input must have a floating-point dtype (float32, the default for the t.* generators, is right). An integer tensor - from t.zeros with dtype int64, say - prints an error in the console and nothing is sent.\n\nmean (drag float, also an inlet):\nThe mean of the LOGARITHMS, not of the values: the median value is e to the power mean. Default 0 (a median of 1).\n\nstd (drag float, also an inlet):\nThe spread of the logarithms. Must be above 0; 0 or below is an error (printed in the console) and nothing is sent. Default 1.\n\nThe parameters are drag floats on the node and also inlets. They are read when the next tensor arrives; setting one does not send. In the demo each slider goes through 't bang float', which passes the number to the parameter first and THEN clicks the t.zeros, so a fresh tensor arrives and is filled at once.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\ntensor out:\nA new tensor, the same shape, dtype and device as the input, filled with fresh independent samples.\n\nTHE HISTOGRAM:\nt.histc divides the range from its min to its max into equal bins and counts how many of the 4096 samples land in each; the bar plot draws the counts, the first bin at the left. Its shape is the shape of the distribution. Samples outside min..max are not counted at all.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\nload_bang clicks t.zeros once when the page opens, and its 64 x 64 tensor of zeros comes back from the node as 4096 samples; the zeros themselves never show, only their shape matters. The heat_map (0 dark, 5 bright) is mostly dark with bright specks. The histogram rises steeply from 0, peaks below 1 and trails off to the right. Raise mean and the shape stretches out; lower std and the tail shortens.\n\nRELATED:\nt.dist.log_normal makes the same samples from a typed shape. t.normal fills with the bell this is built on; t.exponential is another positive fill."
        },
